        "id": 1006497
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-12-05T13:24:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What\u0027s the rationale behind the decision to not call this mode \"native\"? Same for all other software displays.",
      "revId": "30b5973a9cc09f67dbc4bfb34c9e71c12215ff35",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "21d97821_0886cdd6",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/display/VirtualDisplayAdapter.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 229,
      "author": {
        "id": 1516942
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-12-05T14:35:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The standard mechanisms we are aware of to convey this information are HDMI (via its EDID, which supports indicating a video sink\u0027s configuration is natively supported), and vendor-based code for built-in/internal displays.\n\nFor other types of displays (such as external wifi connected displays), we have not identified any other reliable source for this information.\n\nThe current design we envision does not mandate having at least one native display mode (an application will likely be expected to pick the highest suitable resolution in such cases), which make me lean toward not providing one by default.\n\nI suspect it does not matter much either way for virtual/software displays, and I am open to defaulting to all those diplay modes being natives.\n\nWhat are your thoughts on this?",
      "parentUuid": "21fddab5_edd1ff73",
      "revId": "30b5973a9cc09f67dbc4bfb34c9e71c12215ff35",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    }
